## Rationale

Selecting all eligible candidates establishes an auditable baseline before any optimization based on model behaviour.

This avoids introducing unsupported assumptions about:

- the required positive-to-negative ratio
- the value of one negative source over another
- the correct sampling budget
- the usefulness of particular hard negatives

These decisions require model-training and error-analysis evidence and therefore belong to later phases or later loops.

## Determinism

The selection is a deterministic function of:

```text
candidate catalog
+
sampling policy
```

Repeated selection from the same inputs produces the same:

- ordered selected candidates
- excluded tile identities
- selection counts
- selection identity
- persisted JSON content

## Consequences

Positive consequences:

- every supervised Loop 1 candidate remains available for the baseline model
- all-ignore candidates remain explicitly excluded
- selection is reproducible and traceable to the complete candidate population
- later dataset versions can compare their selection decisions with the Loop 1 baseline
- sampling changes can be measured rather than hidden inside runtime logic

Trade-offs:

- the selected population is not class-balanced
- overlapping candidates may contain correlated pixel observations
- the selection is not optimized for model performance
- the small number of positive candidates may limit model generalization

These trade-offs are accepted because Loop 1 is establishing a baseline rather than a final sampling strategy.

## Alternatives Considered

### Random Sampling

Rejected because it would remove eligible evidence without an established sampling budget and would require additional random-state controls.

### Fixed Positive-to-Negative Ratio

Rejected because no model evidence yet demonstrates an appropriate ratio for this study area.

### Hard-Negative-First Sampling

Rejected as the default Loop 1 policy because the relative training value of hard negatives has not yet been measured.

### Treat All-Ignore Candidates as Negative

Rejected because it violates the approved supervision semantics.

